data.content:
Here's what changed:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

1. **`TimeProvider`** is built into .NET 8's BCL — zero new dependencies for production code.
2. The **optional parameter** (`clock = null`) means existing callers don't change at all.
3. In tests, you can use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, ...)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-365));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, ...)));
```

Apply the same pattern (`TimeProvider` via constructor with a default) to every other class that reads `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
